\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Vanishing periods are coboundaries}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{On the torus of \ref{fdip-00OF}, a cocycle whose periods all vanish
is the coboundary of a vertex cochain.}

\proof{
\p{On a cycle, #{d=1}, there are no squares. Set #{X(0)=0} and
#{X(t+1)=X(t)+a(e_1(t))}. The value at #{t=N} equals the period.
That period is #{0}, so #{X} descends to the cycle and has
coboundary #{a}.}

\p{Suppose the statement is known in dimension #{d-1}, and let #{a}
be a cocycle on the #{d}-torus with every period #{0}. At each fixed
last coordinate #{t}, the edges of directions #{1} through #{d-1}
form a cocycle on a torus of dimension #{d-1}. Its periods equal the
global periods, hence vanish. The inductive hypothesis supplies
#{f_t} on that slice with #{f_t(0)=0} and with coboundary equal to
the slice of #{a}. Set
##{\eta(y,t)=a(e_d(y,t))-\bigl(f_{t+1}(y)-f_t(y)\bigr).}
The square in directions #{i} and #{d} rearranges to
##{a(e_d(y+\varepsilon_i,t))-a(e_d(y,t))
=a(e_i(y,t+1))-a(e_i(y,t)).}
The right side equals the same difference of slice coboundaries, so
#{\eta(y+\varepsilon_i,t)=\eta(y,t)}. Thus #{\eta} depends only on
#{t}. The normalization #{f_t(0)=0} makes that value equal to
#{a(e_d(0,t))}. The sum over #{t} is the last period, which is
#{0}, so the cycle case supplies #{c} with
#{c(t+1)-c(t)=a(e_d(0,t))}. For #{X(y,t)=f_t(y)+c(t)} the slice
directions reproduce #{a}, and the last direction reproduces #{a}
because
#{f_{t+1}(y)-f_t(y)+a(e_d(0,t))=a(e_d(y,t))}.}
}
